# ember

A read-only alert reporter for a single server. Ember polls host and Docker
metrics, evaluates threshold alerts, has an LLM agent triage what fired (using
sandboxed shell and read-only Docker tools), and sends a structured report to
your sinks. Nothing listens for replies — every sink is one-way.

## Sinks

- **discord** — webhook. Create one in your notification channel
  (Settings → Integrations → Webhooks) and set `[discord] webhook_url`.
  Reports arrive as embeds, color-coded by severity.
- **email** — plain SMTP, STARTTLS by default (`[email]` section).
- slack / telegram — not implemented; toggles are reserved in `[sinks]`.

## Configuration

Copy `config.example.toml` to `config.toml` in the repo root and fill it in.
Secrets live only in `config.toml`, which is gitignored.

### Prompts

The agent's instructions are every `*.md` file in `config/prompts/` plus
`config/defaults/prompts/`, concatenated in sorted filename order — no
filenames are hardcoded. A file in `config/prompts/` (gitignored) overrides
the same-named default wholesale; nothing is copied or merged at startup, so
`git pull` can never overwrite your customizations.

`config/defaults/prompts/triage.md` is the out-of-the-box triage prompt;
override it by creating your own `config/prompts/triage.md`, or add new
`*.md` files alongside it.

## Tests

    uv run pytest

Tests mock external calls; no API keys, Docker daemon, or notification services required.

## Run

    docker compose up -d --build

or locally:

    uv run python main.py

The container runs with a read-only root filesystem, a bounded `noexec` `/tmp`,
dropped capabilities (keeping only `SETUID`, `SETGID`, and `KILL`),
`no-new-privileges`, `/` mounted read-only at `/host`, and `config/prompts`
mounted read-only. Both the Docker socket and `config.toml` are mounted inside
a root-only directory (`/run/ember`, mode `0700`) so the daemon and read-only
`docker_*` tools can use them, while shell commands run as UID `10001` with a
clean environment.

Shell commands can still read anything world-readable on the host through
`/host`. Make `config.toml` root-owned and private on the host
(`sudo chown root:root config.toml && sudo chmod 600 config.toml`; the
container can't bypass file permissions), and keep the host Docker
socket at its default permissions (`0660 root:docker`): if it is
world-writable, shell commands can reach it at `/host/var/run/docker.sock`.
